// The event behind navigator.domicile.onappcursor.
//
// ITS OWN INTERFACE BECAUSE THE CURSOR IS AN ENUM. `DomicileAppEvent` carries
// the five events that differ only in which window they name, and it carried
// this one too while `cursor` was a `DOMString`: the other five passed the
// empty string and a shell read `""` as "not a cursor event". A
// `DomicileCursorShape` cannot hold `""` -- that is the whole point of a
// closed set -- so keeping one interface would have meant a nullable
// attribute, which is the `""` sentinel again in a shape that reads as
// deliberate. Splitting says the same thing in the type system: an event that
// carries a cursor is a different event from one that does not.

[
    Exposed=Window,
    RuntimeEnabled=DomicileControlChannel
] interface DomicileAppCursorEvent : Event {
  constructor(DOMString type, optional DomicileAppCursorEventInit eventInitDict = {});

  // Which window. The same id an <app> element's app-id names.
  readonly attribute DOMString appId;

  // What the client asked to be shown over it, as the CSS `cursor` keyword to
  // assign. See domicile_cursor_shape.idl for why this is an enum.
  readonly attribute DomicileCursorShape cursor;

  // When the browser process had this message, on `performance.now()`'s
  // clock.
  //
  // NOT `timeStamp`, which is when this object was constructed -- in this
  // renderer, at dispatch -- so subtracting it from itself reports a few
  // microseconds of Blink and calls it the IPC. The difference between the two
  // IS the stage: the read off the compositor's socket, the mojo message, the
  // hop into this process and the dispatch. See
  // components/domicile/mojom/control_channel.mojom.
  readonly attribute DOMHighResTimeStamp arrival;
};
